WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:07.040
﻿Lots of people seem to think that I’m not real.&nbsp;
Apparently I’m AI generated! Maybe it’s true!

00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:11.480
There’s a lot of talk about how artificial&nbsp;
intelligence is going to change the world&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.480 --> 00:00:16.400
in the coming years. A lot of jobs that&nbsp;
people do today will probably disappear,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:19.120
and a lot of the skills that we&nbsp;
think are important to learn will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.120 --> 00:00:23.640
probably become irrelevant, because&nbsp;
AI will be better at them than humans.

00:00:23.640 --> 00:00:26.920
How about the skill of being able&nbsp;
to speak another language? Will it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.920 --> 00:00:29.960
become irrelevant? In some ways it probably will.

00:00:29.960 --> 00:00:49.400
 

00:00:55.320 --> 00:00:58.360
You didn’t know I was fluent&nbsp;
in Turkish, did you? Actually,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.360 --> 00:01:03.400
I’m not. That wasn’t me speaking Turkish.&nbsp;
That was AI. I was speaking English and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.400 --> 00:01:17.440
AI tool translated it into Turkish. Let’s see that&nbsp;
again - and pay attention to how my mouth moves.

00:01:17.440 --> 00:01:21.720
You can see that the AI tool is producing&nbsp;
a Turkish translation in my own voice,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.720 --> 00:01:26.680
and it even controls the movements of my mouth to&nbsp;
make it look like it’s really me that’s speaking.

00:01:26.680 --> 00:01:30.280
The specific tool that I’m using is&nbsp;
called HeyGen. The way it works is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.280 --> 00:01:34.315
that you can upload a video of yourself and&nbsp;
it translates it into a different language.

00:01:56.360 --> 00:02:01.640
You get the point. I’m sure the results are not&nbsp;
perfect, but they seem pretty good. If you’re a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.640 --> 00:02:06.360
native speaker of Turkish, Chinese, or German,&nbsp;
what do you think of the translation? Does it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.360 --> 00:02:10.520
seem authentic, or at least pretty good?&nbsp;
Let us know in the comments down below.

00:02:10.520 --> 00:02:14.440
So, this AI tool doesn’t translate&nbsp;
your speech in real time - you need&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.440 --> 00:02:18.400
to first record a video of yourself, then&nbsp;
upload it and the tool will translate it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.120 --> 00:02:23.080
So it’s useful if you want to create&nbsp;
an on-demand video in another language,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.080 --> 00:02:29.840
but not useful for conversing in real time - yet.&nbsp;
In the future I’m 100% sure that will be possible.

00:02:29.840 --> 00:02:33.560
It’s already possible in text, with&nbsp;
automatically translated captions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.560 --> 00:02:46.400
in meeting software like Zoom and Microsoft&nbsp;
Teams. You can speak in your native language,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.400 --> 00:02:50.360
and other participants will see translated&nbsp;
captions of what you say in their own&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.360 --> 00:02:55.280
native language, in real time (or in almost&nbsp;
real time - there’s a very slight delay).

00:02:55.280 --> 00:02:59.120
The day will come when you will be able to speak&nbsp;
one language, and the other participants will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.120 --> 00:03:03.640
hear your voice speaking a different language&nbsp;
of their choice. This will happen. And you can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.640 --> 00:03:07.880
imagine how that will make foreign language&nbsp;
skills less important for getting a job.

00:03:07.880 --> 00:03:11.320
But that’s online. When meeting people&nbsp;
face to face, you’ll still need to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.320 --> 00:03:15.200
able to speak a common language&nbsp;
- or have an interpreter - RIGHT?

00:03:15.200 --> 00:03:19.080
No, I don’t think so. A few years from&nbsp;
now, I think wearable devices like Meta&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.080 --> 00:03:23.960
smart glasses or Apple Vision Pro will be&nbsp;
able to translate on screen in real time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.960 --> 00:03:29.760
so while speaking to someone face-to-face&nbsp;
you’ll see translated subtitles in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.760 --> 00:03:35.160
glasses. And there will probably be headsets or&nbsp;
earpieces that automatically translate the audio.

00:03:35.160 --> 00:03:39.480
In the more distant future, I think humans will&nbsp;
have the option to have a device inside their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.480 --> 00:03:44.760
brain that makes them able to understand foreign&nbsp;
languages - so they won’t even need to wear geeky&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.760 --> 00:03:49.920
glasses and headsets. I think this will be&nbsp;
a function of Neuralink devices or similar.

00:03:49.920 --> 00:03:52.760
Let’s go over to Twitter and&nbsp;
ask Elon Musk what he thinks.

00:03:52.760 --> 00:03:53.440
Sorry…X!

00:03:54.320 --> 00:03:57.960
SO - will this kind of technology completely&nbsp;
kill language learning? Will people stop&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.960 --> 00:04:03.160
learning languages (aside from their native&nbsp;
language)? NO, I don’t think so! Not entirely.

00:04:03.160 --> 00:04:08.360
I think that AI translation and AI enhanced&nbsp;
language ability will be very useful for work,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.360 --> 00:04:12.680
and very useful for functional use, so people&nbsp;
won’t really need to learn languages to complete&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.680 --> 00:04:13.560
tasks. Translation and interpreting in particular,&nbsp;
will probably no longer be career options.

00:04:13.560 --> 00:04:17.440
But people will continue to learn languages&nbsp;
(a) for enjoyment, (b) to socialize,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.440 --> 00:04:20.240
and (c ) to gain deeper&nbsp;
insight into other cultures.

00:04:20.240 --> 00:04:23.480
Take me for example. I’ve never&nbsp;
learned a language mainly for work,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.480 --> 00:04:28.360
or mainly to accomplish tasks. I’ve learned&nbsp;
languages because it’s fascinating to discover&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.360 --> 00:04:33.200
how a different language works; it gives&nbsp;
me a deep ability to focus on one thing;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.200 --> 00:04:36.520
and because I enjoy talking to&nbsp;
people in languages I’m learning.

00:04:36.520 --> 00:04:41.000
Think of all those times when you spoke another&nbsp;
language (or even made a poor attempt at it)&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.000 --> 00:04:45.600
and the other person’s eyes lit up with&nbsp;
excitement, appreciation, and endearment.

00:04:45.600 --> 00:04:49.520
They’re thrilled to hear you speak their&nbsp;
language. It completely breaks the ice and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.520 --> 00:04:53.040
creates a very memorable and special&nbsp;
moment between you and those people.

00:04:53.040 --> 00:04:57.840
Do you think people will react the same&nbsp;
way if you’re using AI translation? No,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.840 --> 00:05:01.800
of course they won’t. What impresses them&nbsp;
and breaks the ice is the fact that you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.800 --> 00:05:05.960
care enough about their culture and language&nbsp;
to invest time and effort into learning it.

00:05:05.960 --> 00:05:08.920
And when you genuinely learn a&nbsp;
language, you can learn idioms,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.920 --> 00:05:14.120
slang, and parts of the local dialect that make&nbsp;
your speech more expressive and personal. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.120 --> 00:05:19.560
think AI will normally use the formal, standard&nbsp;
language and have trouble with dialectal speech,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.560 --> 00:05:24.240
with different levels of formality, and with the&nbsp;
way people of different ages and social groups&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.240 --> 00:05:28.680
speak. Those subtleties are often what helps&nbsp;
you connect with native speakers the most,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.680 --> 00:05:31.920
but AI will probably not be&nbsp;
good at that for a long time.

00:05:31.920 --> 00:05:35.800
So, I’m sure people will continue to learn&nbsp;
languages because they love having those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.800 --> 00:05:38.640
memorable experiences of connecting with people.

00:05:38.640 --> 00:05:43.120
And as we learned from the failure of Google&nbsp;
Glass, if you walk around in public wearing geeky,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.120 --> 00:05:47.040
privacy-invading smart glasses, people&nbsp;
probably won’t walk to talk to you at all!

00:05:47.040 --> 00:05:50.760
Some people learn languages and don’t even&nbsp;
speak to people in them. They use them mainly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.760 --> 00:05:55.520
for passive consumption, like watching the&nbsp;
news or movies, reading books, or whatever.

00:05:55.520 --> 00:05:59.600
I mean, I like speaking to people (sometimes),&nbsp;
but I’m mainly an introvert, and there are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.600 --> 00:06:04.720
several languages I’ve learned in the past that&nbsp;
I haven’t spoken to anyone in years, like Hebrew,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.720 --> 00:06:09.560
Indonesian, and French. But I often listen to the&nbsp;
news in those languages, or read some articles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.560 --> 00:06:15.080
or listen to podcasts. that kind of thing. Do I&nbsp;
need to? No, I could watch other news in English,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.080 --> 00:06:20.160
and I could listen to different podcasts. But&nbsp;
I enjoy it. I love the feeling of focusing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.160 --> 00:06:25.760
on something and forcing myself to be in the&nbsp;
moment so I can understand as much as possible.

00:06:25.760 --> 00:06:31.320
So even if AI translation makes it possible&nbsp;
to consume that content in perfectly natural&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.320 --> 00:06:35.760
English, I’m still going to watch it in the&nbsp;
original language most of the time. Honestly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.760 --> 00:06:39.160
sitting back with a coffee and watching&nbsp;
the news in a foreign language is one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.160 --> 00:06:42.280
of my favorite things to do when&nbsp;
I have a little bit of downtime.

00:06:42.280 --> 00:06:47.640
I also like reading ancient forms of languages&nbsp;
like Biblical Hebrew and Biblical Aramaic. Even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.640 --> 00:06:51.840
though I can read English translations,&nbsp;
I love the experience of deciphering the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.840 --> 00:06:56.240
text and figuring out what things mean. I&nbsp;
feel like I’m entering a time machine and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.240 --> 00:07:00.640
people from the past are speaking directly&nbsp;
to me in their ancient tongue. It’s already&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.640 --> 00:07:04.200
completely non-essential, and I’ll continue&nbsp;
doing it strictly because it’s incredibly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.200 --> 00:07:08.800
interesting to me. AI is irrelevant&nbsp;
to that kind of language learning.

00:07:08.800 --> 00:07:13.320
In short, learning languages will become more&nbsp;
about human connection than about functional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.320 --> 00:07:18.360
use. Completing tasks and functions won’t&nbsp;
require us to know them. But meaningful and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.360 --> 00:07:23.280
authentic social interaction will be enhanced&nbsp;
by real knowledge and use of the language.

00:07:23.280 --> 00:07:26.800
And there will always be people who learn&nbsp;
languages just because they love them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.800 --> 00:07:30.760
even if they don’t speak them socially&nbsp;
or need them for work. Think about me and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.760 --> 00:07:35.160
this channel - do I have any real reason&nbsp;
to examine the structure of Hungarian or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.160 --> 00:07:39.360
compare the phonology of Russian&nbsp;
and European Portuguese? No! I’m&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.360 --> 00:07:43.640
just a language geek and languages are&nbsp;
cool. AI can’t take that away from me!

00:07:43.640 --> 00:07:48.200
How about you? Will you still dedicate time to&nbsp;
learning and practicing other languages if AI&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.200 --> 00:07:52.800
translation makes it unnecessary? Leave&nbsp;
your answers in the comments down below!

